	(text ""
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 228.346 101.854 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 0.762 0.762)
			)
			(justify left)
		)
		(uuid "0201c7b9-6f22-45ad-8b20-34c68511e9f9")
	)
	(text "The VREF input of the DRV8874 needs an analog volatge, as reference to set the \"Current Threshold\" for the user programmable max current regulated.\nThe analog voltage is generated by PWM + Low Pass filter because PWM can be generated by any microcontroller, while the DAC may be found only or more advanced\nmicrocontrollers. In order to write the FW driver in a general way, the PWM way was chosen.\n\nThe formulation for the current threshold I_TRIP is:\n\n    I_TRIP = VREF / (A_IPROPI * R_IPROPI)\n    Where:\n        - A_IPROPI = Current sensing gain (450 uA/A, typical)\n        - R_IPROPI = Current sensing resistor (2.49 kOhm)\n\nAs good design practice, the Low Pass filter (and R_IPROPI) is designed in order to not introduce significant error in the Current Threshold definition, in\ncomparison with the  one introduced by IPROPI tollerance, which is given and can not be controlled (obviously considering a non-calibrated DRV8874).\n\nWith the deterministic model, the total relative error formulation is the sum of relative error on each input variable:\n\n    e_I_TRIP = e_VREF + e_A_IPROPI + e_R_IPROPI\n\nThis computation is heavily dependent on the nominal value of the formula parameter, so here a nominal case with 2.5A Current Threshold is analyzed. In the\nreport, this design will be analyzed across all the possibile currents, showing the behaviour of the error across all the possible settings that an user can\ndefine.\n\n    At I_TRIP = 2.5A ---> VREF = 2.5 * 450e-6 * 2490 = 2.801V\n\nAccording to the DRV8874 datasheet, the maximum IPROPI sensing error in the 2A-4A range is +/-5.5%.\nThe design goal is to keep each controllable error contribution at least 10 times smaller than this value.\n\n    e_IPROPI = 5.5% ---> e_R_IPROPI_MAX = e_VREF_MAX = 0.55%\n\nThe maximum allowed VREF error at the nominal operating point is:\n\n    Delta_VREF_MAX = 2.801 * 0.0055 = 15.41mV\n\nAssuming an ideal 20kHz PWM with 3.3V amplitude, the required duty cycle is D = 2.801/3.3 = 84.88%.\nAllocating the entire VREF error budget to ripple gives V_RIPPLE_PP_MAX = 2*15.41 = 30.82mVpp.\n\nFor a first-order RC filter (F_PWM >> F_CUTOFF):\n\n    V_RIPPLE_PP ~= V_PWM * D*(1-D) / (F_PWM*R*C)\n    R*C >= V_PWM * D*(1-D) / (F_PWM*V_RIPPLE_PP_MAX) = 0.685ms\n\nThe selected filter components are R = 10kOhm and C = 100nF, giving:\n\n    Tau = 1ms ---> F_CUTOFF = 159.2Hz ---> T_SETTLING(5*Tau) = 5ms\n    V_RIPPLE_PP ~= 21.2mV ---> e_VREF_RIPPLE ~= 0.378%\n\nThe ripple contribution satisfies the 0.55% target at the nominal operating point. The remaining VREF error budget must account for static errors and input\nloading.\n\nNOTE 1! The PWM duty-cycle quantization error is neglected in this preliminary analysis, assuming that the microcontroller timer provides sufficient\nresolution to make its contribution negligible compared to the intrinsic IPROPI sensing error. This assumption will be verified during firmware configuration.\n\nNOTE 2! The filter is designed to introduce a small error, accepting a slower response as a tradeoff. This is reasonable because VREF is a configuration\nparameter, expected to be set during initialization and updated only when the user changes the current threshold. Therefore, fast settling is not required.\n\nNOTE 3! The VREF input bias current may introduce a static voltage error due to the voltage drop across the RC filter resistor. This error depends on the input\ncharacteristics of the DRV8874 and is given by:\n\n    Delta_VREF_BIAS = I_VREF_BIAS * R_FILTER\n\nThe DRV8874 datasheet does not specify the VREF input bias current, so a guaranteed worst-case error cannot be calculated. A complete characterization would\nrequire measurements or additional information from Texas Instruments."
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 163.83 218.948 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 0.762 0.762)
			)
			(justify left)
		)
		(uuid "10afdef5-ce22-452d-b3ac-42adefde1a57")
	)
	(text "3V3 as VCC is okay even if the \ninput A can reach 5V because \nthe buffer is overvoltage \ntollerant up to 5.5V at the \ninput pin A."
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 122.174 188.468 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 0.762 0.762)
			)
			(justify left)
		)
		(uuid "1a8342f4-cdb4-4e0a-9938-490f9ca0226d")
	)
	(text "Signal statuses:\n	- HiZ:	Interlock Open, \n			Motor shall be turned off\n	- 12V:	Interlock closed, \n			Motor CAN be powered on"
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 30.48 182.88 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 0.762 0.762)
			)
			(justify left)
		)
		(uuid "25db59ef-db92-4a43-95f3-01d151c9b0a9")
	)
	(text "Motor Current Anti-Aliasing filter"
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 286.004 159.766 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 1.524 1.524)
				(thickness 0.254)
				(bold yes)
			)
		)
		(uuid "6934986c-b9bc-44b0-9ce3-9cff4b0c1c60")
	)
	(text "The nSLEEP input of the DRV8874 must be controlled by both the FW, during normal operation,\nand the HW Interlock Circuit, to enforce the fail-safe condition. The FW directly controls\nnSLEEP through an Open-Drain output, while the 5V Interlock signal is converted into a\ncompatible Open-Drain signal using a non-inverting buffer.\n\nBoth outputs are connected to nSLEEP, with a common pull-up to 3.3V, implementing\na wired-OR of active-low disable requests without additional logic gates. In practice,\nboth controls can force the H-Bridge into sleep mode independently from the other one.\n\nThe resulting behaviour is:\n\n    FW          INTERLOCK       nSLEEP       DRIVER\n    Hi-Z        Hi-Z            HIGH         Enabled\n    GND         Hi-Z            LOW          Sleep\n    Hi-Z        GND             LOW          Sleep\n    GND         GND             LOW          Sleep\n\nThe Interlock signal is supplied by the regulated 5V power supply of the Road Side PCB.\nThe SN74LVC1G07 is supplied at 3.3V but features overvoltage-tolerant inputs, allowing\nthe 5V Interlock signal to be connected directly without a resistive voltage divider.\nAccording to the buffer datasheet:\n    - VIH_MIN = 2.0V\n    - VIL_MAX = 0.8V\n    - VIN_MAX = 5.5V (recommended operating limit)\n\nThe 5V power supply is assumed to have a tolerance of +/-2%, to be verified during\nthe power supply design. Additional voltage drops may occur along the Interlock\nwiring due to its resistance and the Relay coil current.\n\nThe considered input voltage range is:\n\n    V_INTERLOCK_MIN = 5.0 * (1 - 0.02) = 4.9V\n    V_INTERLOCK_MAX = 5.0 * (1 + 0.02) = 5.1V\n\nSince the entire voltage range is compatible with the SN74LVC1G07 input requirements,\nno voltage divider is required. The direct connection provides the maximum possible\nmargin against voltage drops along the Interlock wiring.\n\nWorst-case verification:\n    - VIN_MIN = 4.9V > VIH_MIN = 2.0V   OK!!\n    - VIN_MAX = 5.1V < VIN_MAX = 5.5V   OK!!\n\nThe minimum INTERLOCK_END voltage required to guarantee a HIGH level is:\n\n    V_INTERLOCK_MIN = VIH_MIN = 2.0V\n\n    --> Max voltage drop allowed along the wiring is:\n        Delta_V_WIRING_MAX = 4.9 - 2.0 = 2.9V\n\nBoth conditions are satisfied, providing a theoretical margin of 2.9V for voltage\ndrops along the Interlock wiring at the minimum power supply voltage.\n\nNOTE! The calculated voltage drop margin guarantees only the logic-level recognition\nof the Interlock signal. The actual maximum allowable drop must also consider the\nminimum operating voltage of the Relay coil, which will be verified separately.\n\nAutomotive transients are assumed to be handled by the upstream 5V power supply.\nIts output voltage limits during such events must be verified during its design."
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 98.552 246.888 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(face "KiCad Font")
				(size 0.762 0.762)
			)
			(justify left)
		)
		(uuid "93eaac1f-3810-4e65-8ab5-2599b4540b8d")
	)
	(text "Interlock state is read here becese the goal is to try and read the\ninterlock effect, which is to open the relay. This read does not guarantee\nto read the actual Interlock level at the input (errors on the delau\ncircuit can happen) or that relay actually opened (faults on MOS or Relay)\ncan happen, but this is just a diagnostic read, it does not try to ensure\nany fault resistance to the circuit. More advanced control needs a SW\ncomparison between the Interlock State at input, Relay Commmand and Relay\nmechanica state, with possibility to open another relay (basically\nensuring redundancy on the power supply opening for the motor)"
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 135.128 144.018 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 0.762 0.762)
			)
			(justify left)
		)
