	(text "H-Bridge DevBoard complete schematics"
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 356.87 211.836 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 1.524 1.524)
				(thickness 0.254)
				(bold yes)
			)
		)
		(uuid "c40e2de7-a65c-4e07-baac-1bb2e22ce393")
	)
	(text "(this schematic won't be part of the PCB but it is placed here\nas reference for the complete schematic used to use the DRV8874)"
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 356.616 237.744 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 0.762 0.762)
			)
		)
		(uuid "ceaa1d90-9581-4d16-ba53-ad4c2f273ccb")
	)
	(text "Considering that the Steering Motor has a direct impact on the safety of the overall vehicle, its power supply must be\ndesigned with fault tolerance in mind, defining a clear fail-safe condition. In particular, the ability to drive the Motor\n(Forward, Reverse or Brake) is tied at HW level to the integrity of the connections required for its correct operation,\nthrough an Interlock Circuit. If the Interlock Circuit is opened (due to a disconnected critical connector, an activated\nSafety Switch or any other interruption), the H-Bridge must be prevented from actively driving the Motor. The defined\nfail-safe condition is Coast, with both H-Bridge outputs in Hi-Z, preventing the Driver from actively applying torque to\nthe Motor.\n\nThis behavior must be enforced at HW level, independently of the FW. For this reason, the main safety component is a\nNormally Open (NO) Relay, which physically disconnects the H-Bridge power supply. In order to reduce the electrical stress\non the Relay during fault conditions (in particular while opening the circuit under load), the Interlock Circuit also\ncontrols the nSLEEP input of the H-Bridge. When the Interlock opens, the H-Bridge is first forced into Sleep Mode, bringing\nboth outputs into Hi-Z through the Driver's internal circuitry. Only after at least 10ms the Relay is commanded to open,\ndisconnecting the power supply when the current drawn by the H-Bridge is expected to be significantly reduced.\n\nThe delay is implemented using an RC network and an analog comparator, with threshold defined by a resistive voltage\ndivider. A series diode prevents the capacitor from discharging back into INTERLOCK_END when the Interlock opens, while\na 47kOhm resistor provides a defined discharge path.\n\nBecause this circuit does not need to produce extremely accurate timings, the threshold voltage can be decided freely,\nand so half the supply voltage is selected, using two equal resistors with 5% Toll. This provides good margins from both\nvoltage extremes and a simple, symmetric reference. 47kOhm resistors are used to reduce the static power consumption\n(acceptable because the voltage divider feeds the high impedance input of a comparator).\n\nDESIGN\n\nWhen the Interlock opens, the diode blocks the reverse current and the capacitor discharges only through R_DISCHARGE.\nBasically the diode is introduced to guarantee that the INTERLOCK_END command to the nSleep pin is by the delay on the\nrelay opening.\nThe delay is calculated using the first-order RC discharge equation:\n\n    V_C(t) = V_INITIAL * exp(-t / (R*C))  --->  t_DELAY = R*C * ln(V_INITIAL / V_TH)\n\nIn order to define the Capacitor value, the conditions producing the shortest delay are considered (in order to guarantee\nthe 10ms delay):\n\n    t_DELAY_MIN = R_DISCHARGE_MIN * C_MIN * ln(V_INITIAL_MIN / V_TH_MAX)\n    --> C_MIN = t_DELAY / (R_DISCHARGE_MIN * ln(V_INITIAL_MIN / V_TH_MAX))\n\n    where:\n        V_INITIAL_MIN     = (4.9 - 0.3) * 44.65 / (44.65 + 1.05) = 4.494V\n        V_TH_MAX          = 5.1 * 49.35 / (44.65 + 49.35) = 2.6775V\n        R_DISCHARGE_MIN   = 47k * (1 - 0.05) = 44.65kOhm\n        C_MIN             = C * (1 - 0.2)                  Cap. with 20% tolerance is used to reduce the costs\n\n    --> C_MIN = 10ms / (44.65k * ln(4.494 / 2.6775)) = 432.4nF  --> C = C_MIN / (1 - 0.2) = 540.5nF\n    --> C = 680nF (to follow the E6 value series)\n    --> t_DELAY_MIN = 44.65k * 0.544uF * ln(4.494 / 2.6775) = 12.58ms > 10ms  OK!!\n\nThe maximum delay is evaluated to define the maximum time needed by the circuit to react to an Interlock opening:\n\n    t_DELAY_MAX = R_DISCHARGE_MAX * C_MAX * ln(V_INITIAL_MAX / V_TH_MIN)\n\n    where:\n        V_INITIAL_MAX = (5.1 - 0.3) * 49.35 / (49.35 + 0.95) = 4.709V\n        V_TH_MIN = 4.9 * 44.65 / (49.35 + 44.65) = 2.3275V\n        R_DISCHARGE_MAX = 47k * (1 + 0.05) = 49.35kOhm\n        C_MAX = 680nF * (1 + 0.2) = 816nF\n\n    --> t_DELAY_MAX = 49.35k * 0.816uF * ln(4.709 / 2.3275) = 28.38ms\n\nNOTE! The calculated times refer to the Relay Driver Circuit switching event. The Relay's electromechanical release does\nnot happen simultaneuly (Max. 10 ms delay from control to opening). In practice, the time delay range beteween Interlock\nOpen event and the Relay opening is: [12.58; 38.38] ms\n\nNOTE! The analysis assumes a constant 0.3V diode drop and Ideal OpAmp iputs. It is highly probable that these phenomena\nhave a negligible effect on the design, but full SPICE simulation is suggested for a Real-World application"
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 14.224 100.584 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 0.762 0.762)
			)
			(justify left)
		)
		(uuid "d15e43ab-b43e-404a-8964-274b2e65b8c8")
	)
